# =============================================================================
# FILE: .env.example
# =============================================================================
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# Documents every environment variable this project understands.
#
# HOW TO USE
# ----------
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is git-ignored. `.env.example` is committed on purpose so that
# anyone cloning the repo can see which variables exist without ever seeing
# your real values.
#
# THE `NEXT_PUBLIC_` PREFIX — READ THIS, IT MATTERS
# -------------------------------------------------
# Next.js has exactly one rule for environment variables:
#
#   * A variable named `NEXT_PUBLIC_SOMETHING` is INLINED INTO THE BROWSER
#     BUNDLE at build time. Anyone who opens DevTools can read it.
#   * A variable WITHOUT that prefix is only readable on the server
#     (inside `app/api/**/route.ts` and Server Components).
#
# So the rule of thumb for a dApp is:
#
#   Public/harmless (chain id, network name, explorer URL) -> NEXT_PUBLIC_
#   Secret (paid RPC keys, API tokens)                     -> no prefix
#
# This project deliberately performs all chain reads on the server precisely so
# that a paid RPC endpoint can stay secret. See `lib/queries.ts`.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. NETWORK SELECTION  (public — the browser needs to know which chain it is on)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which Injective network the app talks to.
#
# Accepted values:
#   testnet  -> chain id `injective-888`. Free test INJ from the faucet.
#               USE THIS FOR THE WORKSHOP.
#   mainnet  -> chain id `injective-1`. Real money. Real losses.
#
# Default if unset: testnet (see `lib/constants.ts`). We default to testnet on
# purpose — the safe option should be the one you get by accident.
NEXT_PUBLIC_INJECTIVE_NETWORK=testnet


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. SERVER-SIDE RPC OVERRIDES  (secret — never prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave these blank to use Injective's free public endpoints, which are derived
# automatically from the network above by `getNetworkEndpoints()`.
#
# Set them when you move to production and buy a dedicated node from a provider
# such as Quicknode, Blockdaemon or Lavender.Five. Public endpoints are rate
# limited and will start returning 429s under workshop-sized load.
#
# Which is which:
#   REST_ENDPOINT — the "LCD" / Cosmos REST gateway (HTTP + JSON). Used for
#                   account lookups, latest block, and broadcasting.
#   GRPC_ENDPOINT — the gRPC-web gateway. Used by the `ChainGrpc*Api` classes
#                   for typed, protobuf-encoded queries such as balances.
#
# Example values for testnet (these are the defaults, shown for illustration):
#   INJECTIVE_REST_ENDPOINT=https://testnet.sentry.lcd.injective.network
#   INJECTIVE_GRPC_ENDPOINT=https://testnet.sentry.chain.grpc-web.injective.network
INJECTIVE_REST_ENDPOINT=
INJECTIVE_GRPC_ENDPOINT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. OPTIONAL UI TUNING  (public)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How often, in milliseconds, the balance and chain-status panels re-fetch.
# Injective produces a block roughly every ~0.65s, but polling that fast will
# get you rate limited. 15s is a comfortable default.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=15000
